<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pindus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pindus Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pindus is the main mountain range of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, running southwards from the Albanian border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a>. Its highest peak, Smolikas, rises to 2,637 metres. In antiquity its valleys were home to peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athamanians">Athamanians</a> and Dolopians, and the range has long formed a barrier between western and eastern Greece.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pindus.jpg" alt="Pindus" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bogdan Giuşcă · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pindus_Mountains_02_bgiu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pindus, also written Pindos or Pindhos, lies in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> and is roughly 160 km long. Because it runs from north to south along much of mainland Greece, it is known colloquially as the spine of Greece. It stretches from near the Greek–Albanian border southwards to the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Geologically it is an extension of the Dinaric Alps.</p><p>Its most notable mountains are Gramos, Smolikas, Vasilitsa, Tymfi or Gamila, Lakmos or Peristeri, the Athamanika or Tzoumerka mountains, Vardousia and the Agrafa mountains. The wide range of altitude produces two main forest zones: conifers, including a subspecies of Austrian pine and the endemic Greek fir, at the highest elevations, and mixed broadleaf forest in the valleys and canyons below. Wolves, jackals and bears live in the forests, and the mountain lakes hold colonies of herons, spoonbills, egrets and pelicans, including the rare Dalmatian pelican.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name has a long history. The 12th-century Byzantine writer John Tzetzes said the range was then called Metzovon. When Anastasios Gordios translated a praise of St Vissarion into a more colloquial language between 1682/83 and 1689, he wrote that a mountain the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Pindos was the same as the one called Metzovon in Barbarian. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> to the 19th century the folk name was Metsovo, or the mountains of Metsovo.</p><p>That name most probably did not cover the whole range as the Pindus does today, but only its central part between the area of Aspropotamos and the springs of the Aoös River, which coincides with the region the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called Pindos. The main groups living in the range are Epirotes, Roumeliotes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlachs">Vlachs</a>. Its traditional economy was pastoral, with sheep raised by Sarakatsani and Aromanian shepherds, and villages such as Perivoli and Smixi include Aromanian communities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Two significant gorges, the Vikos Gorge and the Aoos Gorge, lie in the range. With the mountain valley of Valia Kalda they form the National Park of Northern Pindos. The Vikos-Aoos National Park, created in 1973, includes Mount Tymfi and both gorges, while the Pindus National Park, also known as Valia Kalda, was established in 1966 and covers some 7,000 hectares of black pine, beech and Bosnian pine. Villages such as Metsovo have developed into tourist resorts with ski facilities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pindus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Athamanians were a people of the <b>Pindus</b> mountains around the upper Achelous, between Epirus, Thessaly and Aetolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-athamania">Kingdom of Athamania</a></li></ul></section>
